Like I said: Instead of directly creating DAOs via 
jdbi.onDemand(MyDAO.class)
 each time they are needed, make the DAO a Spring 
@Bean
 and inject it wherever it is needed. This way, Spring AOP can intercept it.


@Configuration
public class MyConfig {
    // ...
    @Bean
    public Jdbi jdbi (DataSource dataSource) {
        Jdbi jdbi = Jdbi.create(dataSource);
        jdbi.installPlugin(new SqlObjectPlugin());
        return jdbi;
    }

    @Bean
    public MyDAO myDAO (Jdbi jdbi) {
        return jdbi.onDemand(MyDAO.class);
    }
}



@Service
public class MyService {
    private final MyDAO myDAO;

    public MyService(MyDAO myDAO) {
        this.myDAO = myDAO;
    }

    public Long getCount() {
        return myDAO.getCount();
    }
}



