The issue of returning a Boolean object no longer occurs.

The issue you're encountering is a classic HTTP parsing problem... When your buffer is large enough to read the entire request in one go, 
input.read(buffer)
 successfully reads all the data, but then the loop continues and calls 
read()
 again. Since the client (curl) has sent everything and is waiting for a response, your server blocks waiting for more data that will never come.


One of clean solution is to ensure your 
request
 object properly tracks:




Whether headers are complete (found 
\r\n\r\n
)




Whether a body is expected (check 
Content-Length
 or 
Transfer-Encoding
 headers)




How many body bytes have been received vs. expected






Then simply don't call 
read()
 again once 
isDone()
 returns true:


byte[] buffer = new byte[1024];
int offset = 0;

while(!request.isDone()) {
    int n = input.read(buffer, offset, buffer.length - offset);
    if(n == -1) break;

    // parseRequest now returns how many bytes it consumed
    int consumed = request.parseRequest(buffer, offset + n);

    if(request.isDone()) {
        // Preserve unconsumed bytes for next request
        int remaining = (offset + n) - consumed;
        if(remaining > 0) {
            System.arraycopy(buffer, consumed, buffer, 0, remaining);
        }
        offset = remaining;
        break;
    }

    offset = 0;
}



For a GET request like your curl example, there's no request body, so once you've parsed the headers (ending with 
\r\n\r\n
), your 
isDone()
 should return 
true
 and you should exit the loop immediately.


The key insight:
 You need to detect completion from the HTTP protocol itself, not from the socket closing or blocking.


Thanks to @Remy Lebeau for pointing out the importance of handling pipelined requests and preserving unconsumed bytes.

