A Key-Sharing dictionary is composed of a shared keys table among 
all instances
 of the same class and a value array that stores the actual values 
per instance
 corresponding to the shared keys.


sys.getsizeof
 measures the memory size of the object passed to it, including its internal structure. As the shared keys table is stored at the class level and is not part of the instance's 
__dict__
, this will return the memory used by the value array but not the shared keys table.


The key-sharing is handled within the 
PyDictObject
 object, see 
dictobject.h
. The member 
ma_values
 can either be 
NULL
 or point to an array. This handles the sharing internally. However, it is still inside a 
PyDictObject
, so it is reported as built-in type 
<class 'dict'>
 from within python.


sys.getsizeof
 only measures the size of the dictionary object 
colors.__dict__
, not the size of the strings it references. The dictionary stores pointers to the strings, and the size of these pointers remains constant regardless of the size of the strings they point to.
Similarly, the size of the dictionary remains the same when replacing 
self.b
 with a long string, because it only stores pointers to the attribute values. The size of the actual string is not included in the result.


Finally, in Python, integers are objects, and the dictionary stores pointers to these objects. The size of the pointer is constant, regardless of whether it points to a string or an integer.


The difference in size is due to changes in the implementation of dictionaries. Python 3.12 introduced a new member type 
PyDictValues
 (was 
PyObject**
) for key-sharing dictionaries, see 
this commit
. These changes increased the base size of dictionaries to accommodate the new structure of the value array.


typedef struct _dictkeysobject PyDictKeysObject;
+++ typedef struct _dictvalues PyDictValues;

/* The ma_values pointer is NULL for a combined table
 * or points to an array of PyObject* for a split table
 */
typedef struct {
    PyObject_HEAD

    /* Number of items in the dictionary */
    Py_ssize_t ma_used;

    /* Dictionary version: globally unique, value change each time
       the dictionary is modified */
    uint64_t ma_version_tag;

    PyDictKeysObject *ma_keys;

    /* If ma_values is NULL, the table is "combined": keys and values
       are stored in ma_keys.

       If ma_values is not NULL, the table is splitted:
       keys are stored in ma_keys and values are stored in ma_values */
---    PyObject **ma_values;
+++    PyDictValues *ma_values;
} PyDictObject;



and


struct _dictvalues {
    uint64_t mv_order;
    PyObject *values[1];
};

