The annotation isn't on the field. Fields cannot be 'null' or 'non-null'.


It's type usage that has that property.


Note that many nullity frameworks are confused about this and get it wrong. Or rather, TYPE_USE is newer than FIELD, and the first release of annotations came with a bevy of nullity marking frameworks and they had no other option. Also, there is an argument to be made to sort it out at the field/param/returntype level. But, the point is, JSpecify does it on the type level.


The 
type use
 of that field is annotated. This allows JSpecify's annotations to e.g. show up on the 
String[]
 in 
String[][]
 or on the 
? extends Whatever
 in 
Map<String, List<? extends Whatever>>
; for an annotation that can only be targeted at e.g. fields, params, and methods - you couldn't do that.


But, that means 
the field
 is not annotated at all. It's the field's type use that is annotated.


But how does javac know?


This:


private @Foo String x;



is ambiguous.


Is that 
@Foo
 annotating the field.. or is it annotating the String type?


Imagine this:


private List<@Foo String> x;



Now there's no question at all: That annotation is annotating 
String
. Obviously.


The way java resolves this ambiguity is to look at the annotation's own definition. JSpecify's 
@Nullable
 says: TYPE_USE. And only TYPE_USE. hence javac knows there's only one way to interpret it that wouldn't lead to an instant error, and decides that's what you meant.


What you're looking for is 
getAnnotatedType()
:


import static java.lang.annotation.ElementType.*;
import static java.lang.annotation.RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME;

import module java.base;

class ReflectionTest {
  void main() throws Exception {
    Field field = FooTU.class.getDeclaredField("bar");
    AnnotatedType type = field.getAnnotatedType();
    System.out.println("FooTU Field: " + field.isAnnotationPresent(TestAnnTypeUse.class));
    System.out.println("FooTU type: " + type.isAnnotationPresent(TestAnnTypeUse.class));

    field = FooF.class.getDeclaredField("bar");
    type = field.getAnnotatedType();
    System.out.println("FooF Field: " + field.isAnnotationPresent(TestAnnField.class));
    System.out.println("FooF type: " + type.isAnnotationPresent(TestAnnField.class));

    field = FooB.class.getDeclaredField("bar");
    type = field.getAnnotatedType();
    System.out.println("FooB Field: " + field.isAnnotationPresent(TestAnnBoth.class));
    System.out.println("FooB type: " + type.isAnnotationPresent(TestAnnBoth.class));
  }

  private record FooTU(@TestAnnTypeUse String bar) {}
  private record FooF(@TestAnnField String bar) {}
  private record FooB(@TestAnnBoth String bar) {}
}

@Documented
@Target(TYPE_USE)
@Retention(RUNTIME)
@interface TestAnnTypeUse  {}

@Documented
@Target(FIELD)
@Retention(RUNTIME)
@interface TestAnnField  {}

@Documented
@Target({FIELD, TYPE_USE})
@Retention(RUNTIME)
@interface TestAnnBoth  {}



Prints:


> java ReflectionTest.java

FooTU Field: false
FooTU type: true
FooF Field: true
FooF type: false
FooB Field: true
FooB type: true



This shows how java interprets that annotation as being on the type, or on the field, or on both, depending on the annotation's definition.

