According to the 
docs
 in the OkHttpClient class, the method 
newBuilder()
 allows to build and create a client that shares the same connection pool, thread pools, and configuration of the original one.




Customize Your Client With newBuilder()


You can customize a shared OkHttpClient instance with [newBuilder]. This builds a client that shares the same connection pool, thread pools, and configuration. Use the builder methods to add configuration to the derived client for a specific purpose.




Regarding your question




In my case the instances are sharing the same inner pools, like OkHttpClient is saying?




Yes, and this can be easily tested by creating two 
OkHttpClient
 instances, where the second is created with 
newBuilder()
 from the first client, and then checking if both of them share the same instance of 
ConnectionPool
.


OkHttpClient okHttpClient1 = new OkHttpClient();
OkHttpClient okHttpClient2 = okHttpClient1.newBuilder().build();

// Prints true
System.out.println(okHttpClient1.connectionPool().equals(okHttpClient2.connectionPool()));



Regarding your last question




Is it good to do this in my case?




Probably not, but I may have misunderstood you case.


From my understanding, you want to have two separate clients that handle disntinctively HTTP requests and WebSockets requests. The fact that you want them to be two separate instances leads me to think that you do not want them to share any state. So, if you truly want to handle those requests separately, you should create two competely independent instances.


public class OkHttpClients {

    private static final OkHttpClient HTTP = new OkHttpClient.Builder()
        .followRedirects(false)
        .readTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
        .retryOnConnectionFailure(true)
        .build();

    private static final OkHttpClient WS = new OkHttpClient.Builder()
        .pingInterval(Duration.ofMinutes(1))
        .readTimeout(Duration.ofMillis(0))
        .connectTimeout(Duration.ofMillis(0))
        .build();

    public static OkHttpClient getHttp(){
        return HTTP;
    }

    public static OkHttpClient getWS(){
        return WS;
    }
}



Keep in mind that the docs 
recommend
 using a single 
OkHttpClient
 for an optimal usage; It is not 
mandatory
.


Instead, if you specifically want the two clients to share their state while having different configurations, you could keep your code as it is.

You could use 
@JsonAlias
 for your case. It allows reading (deserializing) the property with any of the specified names, but uses the 
"real"
 name for writing (serializing).


public class PredictTimeDO {
    @JsonAlias("ShipperCode")
    private String shipperCode;
    @JsonAlias("SendProvince")
    private String sendProvince;
    @JsonAlias("SendCity")
    private String sendCity;

    //etc...
}

Looking at the 
source code
, the implementation of 
MemorySegment::getString
 essentially does the same thing as when manually copying the string bytes as demonstrated 
here
:




byte[] bytes = new byte[length];
MemorySegment.copy(segment, JAVA_BYTE, offset, bytes, 0, length);
return new String(bytes, charset);





Except that approach knows the length ahead of time, so it will be slightly faster for longer strings since the string won't have to be searched for a null character (which 
getString
 has to do).


There has to be at least one copy since you have to get a 
byte[]
 from the 
MemorySegment
. With the provided public API, there has to be at least another copy to ensure the integrity of 
String
 (i.e., don't want to let anyone modify the 
byte[]
 internal to the string). And then on top of that, the bytes may have to be decoded and then encoded to "fit" the internal representation of string (which since Java 9 uses Latin1 when possible and UTF-16 when not).


The only way I can think of to 
potentially
 avoid one of those copies is to use the internal 
JavaLangAccess
 API. Though as you'll see in the benchmarks below, this is not a magic bullet in all cases. And it also behaves differently. The 
String
 constructor replaces malformed/unmappable input, whereas 
JavaLangAccess
 will throw a 
CharacterCodingException
. Plus, as noted, it's 
internal
 API.




Benchmarks (JMH)


I found the performance can be quite dependent on the encoding used and the content of the string. I also tested using a 
CharsetDecoder
 as suggested by g00se.


I ran the benchmarks using two strings, one which contains only Latin characters (actually only English characters, specifically Lorem Ipsum text) and one which contains Unicode characters (taken from 
https://www.w3.org/2001/06/utf-8-test/UTF-8-demo.html
). These strings were 7,308 and 7,216 characters (
char
) in length, respectively.


I used an 8 core 7th gen Intel CPU (I know, it's old). Configured 5 warmups (10 seconds) and 10 measurements (15 seconds). Only 1 fork. Java 24.0.1 and JMH 1.37.


Note for the US-ASCII and ISO-8859-1 encodings, processing the Unicode text obviously results in encoding/decoding errors. The benchmarks still run but technically the text is copied incorrectly. This may make the results invalid in these cases specifically. The fact the same two strings are used repeatedly may also affect the results.


Also note that using 
JavaLangAccess
 requires the following at compile-time and run-time:


--add-exports java.base/jdk.internal.access=ALL-UNNAMED



Results


Here's are the different "types" (i.e., approaches).








Type


Description










Direct


Used 
MemorySegment::getString






Manual


Used 
MemorySegment::copy
 and 
new String(...)






Access


Used 
MemorySegment::copy
 and 
JavaLangAccess






Decoder


Used 
CharsetDecoder








