String _ = "Hello World"
 does not declare a variable named 
_
. It simply evaluates the expression 
"Hello World"
, and that's it. 
_
 is not used as an identifier here.


This is the feature 
Unnamed Variables & Patterns (JEP 456)
, which is implemented in Java 22, and available as a preview feature in Java 21. The code would not compile in Java 17.


This feature is useful if you only need the side effect of evaluating an expression, or if the syntax requires you to declare a variable (e.g. in an enhanced 
for
 loop), but you don't actually need that variable. In such cases, you can replace the variable name with the 
keyword

_
.


Here are some examples from the motivation section in JEP 456:


static int count(Iterable<Order> orders) {
    int total = 0;
    // could be written as 'for (Order _ : orders)' instead, since order is not used
    for (Order order : orders)
        total++;
    return total;
}



