Gatherers are not a preview feature anymore in Java 24. The issue is that you're using a non-compatible lambda.


Gatherer
 is a functional interface, but the single method you must implement is 
integrator()
. That requires no arguments. The integrator itself can also be defined as a lambda and takes not 2 but 3 arguments: the intermediate state, the element, and the downstream. In other words (and since the intermediate state is not used, let's make that clear using 
_
):


Stream.of("apple", "banana", "kiwi").gather(() -> (_, word, downstream) -> {
    for (char ch : word.toCharArray()) {
        if (Character.isLowerCase(ch)) {
            downstream.push(ch);
        }
    }
    return true;
}).forEach(System.out::println);



Slightly improved to make more use of a possible 
false
 return of 
downstream.push
 (no need to push if the downstream doesn't want more data):


Stream.of("apple", "banana", "kiwi").gather(() -> (_, word, downstream) -> word.chars()
        .filter(Character::isLowerCase)
        .allMatch(ch -> downstream.push((char) ch))
).forEach(System.out::println);



Now, that doesn't look pretty with the nested lambda, so I'd use the static factory method:


Stream.of("apple", "banana", "kiwi").gather(Gatherer.of((_, word, downstream) -> word.chars()
        .filter(Character::isLowerCase)
        .allMatch(ch -> downstream.push((char) ch))
)).forEach(System.out::println);



(Note that I'm not using 
Integrator.of
 because I don't see much use in it here. I'm also not making the integrator greedy because it simply doesn't need to be.)

I've actually solved this by generating proxy functions in 
_bind.cpp
 files. For instance, in 
message1_bind.cpp
 I've defined a function 
void init_message1(pyinit11::module& m)
 and then in 
main_bind.cpp
 I call them all inside 
PYBIND11_MODULE(protocol_name, m)
 so I only have only one 
PYBIND11_MODULE()
 call. Here's a minimal example to describe the method better:


message1_bind.cpp
:


#include <pybind11/pybind11.h>
#include "messages/message_1.h"

void init_message1(pybind11::module& m)
{
    pybind11::class_<protocol_name::Message1>(m, "Message1").def(pybind11::init<>());
    m.def("serialize_message1", &protocol_name::serialize_message_1);
    m.def("deserialize_message1", &protocol_name::deserialize_message_1);
}



And then inside 
main_bind.cpp


#include <pybind11/pybind11.h>
#include "message1_bind.cpp"
/// ... all other includes

PYBIND11_MODULE(protocol_name, m)
{
    init_message1(m);
    /// init_message2(m) and so on..
}

tl;dr


By far the best performance in this case, on a multi-core machine, is a parallel stream to generate 
IntSummaryStatistics
 object.


IntSummaryStatistics stats = 
    integers                              // Your `List` of `Integer` objects. 
        .stream()                         // Make a stream of the `Integer` objects.
        .mapToInt( Integer :: intValue )  // Convert each `Integer` object into an `int` primitive value, via an `IntStream`. 
        .parallel()                       // Process the `IntStream` broken up into parallel tasks to leverage multiple CPU cores. 
        .summaryStatistics();             // Examine every `int` element in the `IntStream` while calculating min, max, sum, average, and count stored in an `IntSummaryStatistics` object.

int min = stats.getMin() ;                // Interrogate the `IntSummaryStatistics` object. 
int max = stats.getMax() ;



Examine source code


Look at the 
Math.java
 source code of Java implementations based on the OpenJDK codebase.


@IntrinsicCandidate
public static long min(long a, long b) {
    return (a <= b) ? a : b;
}



@IntrinsicCandidate
public static long max(long a, long b) {
    return (a >= b) ? a : b;
}



Those methods use a 
ternary operator
, a briefer alternative to an 
if
 statement.


So we see that calling these methods is basically doing the same work as your 
if
 code.


Technically your second code does a bit more. Your second code always assigns a value to the primitive variable whether it needs to or not. But I cannot imagine that a primitive value assignment would ever be a bottleneck in your app.


👉🏽 Beware of falling into the trap of 
premature optimization
. Before any optimization attempt, measure performance empirically.


Collections.min
 & 
Collections.max


Personally, I would just use the utility methods 
min
 & 
max
 provided on the 
Collections
 class. Clean, simple, fool-proof.


int min = Collections.min( myNumbers ) ;
int max = Collections.max( myNumbers ) ;



See the 
source code
 for the OpenJDK implementation. That code uses as 
Iterator
. That may explain why this approach seems to be over three times slower than the other two approaches tested in code below.


Stream


See 
Comment by g00se
 for yet another approach using Java streams with 
Collectors.summarizingInt
 to produce 
IntSummaryStatistics
.


System.out.println( list.stream().collect(Collectors.summarizingInt(n->n)) );



Example code


Let's try each of those approaches.


Get some sample data. We use a collection of twenty million numbers as input.


List < Integer > integers =
        IntStream
                .generate(
                        ( ) -> ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt( 1 , 1_000_000 )
                )
                .limit( 20_000_000 )
                .boxed()
                .toList();



Try each of our three approaches, multiple times. And let's put a spin on the last of those three, trying it parallelized with a call to 
parallel
 on this 10-core machine.


for ( int index = 0 ; index < 10 ; index++ )
{
    System.out.println( "Loop ordinal " + ( index + 1 ) );

    long x = System.nanoTime();
    // ➡️ Approach used in Question.
    int min = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
    int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
    for ( int currentNum : integers )
    {
        if ( currentNum < min ) min = currentNum;
        if ( currentNum > max ) max = currentNum;
    }
    Duration xDuration = Duration.ofNanos( System.nanoTime() - x );
    IO.println( "min = " + min + " | max = " + max + " | xDuration = " + xDuration );

    long y = System.nanoTime();
    // ➡️ Simplest approach, using utility class bundled with Java.
    min = Collections.min( integers );
    max = Collections.max( integers );
    Duration yDuration = Duration.ofNanos( System.nanoTime() - y );
    IO.println( "min = " + min + " | max = " + max + " | yDuration = " + yDuration );

    long z = System.nanoTime();
    // ➡️ Using Java streams approach suggested by g00se.
    // IntSummaryStatistics stats = integers.stream().mapToInt( Integer :: intValue ).summaryStatistics();
    IntSummaryStatistics stats = integers.stream().collect( Collectors.summarizingInt( n -> n ) );
    min = stats.getMin();
    max = stats.getMax();
    Duration zDuration = Duration.ofNanos( System.nanoTime() - z );
    IO.println( "min = " + min + " | max = " + max + " | zDuration = " + zDuration );
}

    long zParallel = System.nanoTime();
    // ➡️ Using Java streams approach suggested by g00se, like the approach above, but adding parallelization.
    // IntSummaryStatistics statsParallel = integers.stream().mapToInt( Integer :: intValue ).parallel().summaryStatistics();
    IntSummaryStatistics statsParallel = integers.stream().parallel().collect( Collectors.summarizingInt( n -> n ) );
    min = statsParallel.getMin();
    max = statsParallel.getMax();
    Duration zDurationParallel = Duration.ofNanos( System.nanoTime() - zParallel );
    IO.println( "min = " + min + " | max = " + max + " | zDurationParallel = " + zDurationParallel );



This is 
not
 a very good benchmark test. But it is likely valid, in the ballpark, if we omit the first run.


I ran this code with Java 25, on macOS Sequoia, on MacBook Pro with Apple Silicon M1 Pro chip, 16 gigs RAM.


Results show that all three approaches take tens of milliseconds for our list of twenty million elements. I am surprised to see:




The non-parallel streams approach seems to be the fastest, by a significant amount. Even more amazing is that the streams approach with 
IntSummaryStatistics
 does the most work, calculating count, average, and sum along with min & max.


Parallelizing the streams approach makes a huge impact, pushing the resulting fractional second time over by a decimal place! We go from streams taking about .02S to dropping to only about .004S. Parallelizing does not always make a great improvement with streams, but it does here in this case. (Using 10-core, 2 efficiency + 8 performance, in Apple Silicon 
M1 Pro
 chip.)




…
Loop ordinal 9
min = 1 | max = 999999 | xDuration = PT0.043894791S
min = 1 | max = 999999 | yDuration = PT0.078488875S
min = 1 | max = 999999 | zDuration = PT0.020022583S
min = 1 | max = 999999 | zDurationParallel = PT0.004057375S
Loop ordinal 10
min = 1 | max = 999999 | xDuration = PT0.046943083S
min = 1 | max = 999999 | yDuration = PT0.081117334S
min = 1 | max = 999999 | zDuration = PT0.019126125S
min = 1 | max = 999999 | zDurationParallel = PT0.003920666S



Again, (a) this is not an excellent benchmark test, and (b) do not worry about looping a list in Java. Surely you must have bigger fish to fry in your app’s real-world performance.

Introduction


Oracle has a helpful tutorial, 
Creating a GUI With Swing
.  Skip the Learning Swing with the NetBeans IDE section.


All Swing applications must start with a call to the 
SwingUtilities

invokeLater
 method.  This method ensures that the Swing components are created and executed on the 
Event Dispatch Thread
.


The code posted in the question was difficult for me to understand.  I restructured the code so it was easier for me to understand.


Here's a picture of the GUI.




This isn't the full game.  It's an example to show how to structure game code.




Left-click on an empty square to place a piece.




Left-click on a piece to reverse its color.






Try it and see.  I didn't use 
JLabels
 for the pieces.  Since the code in the question had 
JPanel
 drawing code, I went ahead and drew the pieces.


Explanation


The first thing I did was create a model class.  A model class is a plain Java getter/settee class.  This allows me to put all the game fields in one place and treat the model as a single object.


I created separate classes for the 
JFrame
 and the drawing 
JPanel
.  This put the 
JFrame
 code in one place and the game board drawing code 
JPanel
 in a separate place.  Like code grouped together in one place is easier to understand and modify.  Small methods and classes are easier to understand and modify.


I created a 
BoardListener
 class extending 
MouseAdapter
 (implementing 
MouseListener
) to listen for mouse presses. Left-clicking on an empty square places a piece.  Left-clicking on a piece changes its color.


Code


Here's the complete runnable code.  I made the additional classes inner classes so I could post the code as one block.  The additional classes should be additional files in a Java project.


