The solution with time complexity - 
O(n * k)
:




Save all subset sums and their the latest start idx


Use previously saved sums and their start idx to calculate new sums for this start idx




public static int solve(int k, List<Integer> list) {
    var sumToStart = new HashMap<Integer, Integer>();
    int minLen = -1;
    for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {
        int cost = list.get(i);
        // 1 is the shortest possible length
        if (cost == k) return 1;

        var newSumToStart = new HashMap<Integer, Integer>();
        for (var entry : sumToStart.entrySet()) {
            int sum = entry.getKey();
            int start = entry.getValue();

            int newSum = sum + cost;
            if (newSum > k) continue;

            // save newLen if it is smaller than previous
            if (newSum == k) {
                int newLen = i - start + 1;
                if (minLen == -1 || newLen < minLen) {
                    minLen = newLen;
                }
                continue;
            }

            var oldStart = sumToStart.get(newSum);
            // do not update newSumToStart, if old start is bigger than current start
            // read the note in the end of the answer
            if (oldStart != null && oldStart > start) continue;
            newSumToStart.put(newSum, start);
        }

        sumToStart.putAll(newSumToStart);
        sumToStart.put(cost, i);
    }

    return minLen;
}



The note:

Why does 
sumToStart
 need to store the latest possible 
start
?

It leads to shorter path that sums to 
k
.

For example:


k = 11
list=[5, 10, 5, 1]



For 
sum
 = 10, 
start
 has to be 1, not 0, because [10, 5, 1] is shorter than [5, 10, 5, 1].

The final state of 
sumToStart={1=3, 5=2, 6=2, 10=1}
.

