You’re right: just swapping in 
SSLServerSocketFactory
 isn’t enough. For HTTPS you need:




a server certificate + private key (in a keystore),




a TLS context that uses that key, and




a certificate the 
browser actually trusts
 (or you’ll get the “not secure” warning).






Below is a minimal, working path for a LAN-only toy server on Android + desktop browser.


1) Generate a cert that matches how you’ll connect


If you type the 
IP address
 in the browser (e.g., 
https://192.168.1.100:8443/test
), your certificate 
must
 have that IP in the Subject Alternative Name (SAN). Browsers ignore 
CN
 and require SAN.


Option A: “Locally-trusted” (best DX on your own machines)


Use 
mkcert
 (it creates a local CA and adds it to your OS trust store, so the browser won’t warn):


mkcert -install
mkcert 192.168.1.100 myphone.local
# produces: 192.168.1.100+1-key.pem and 192.168.1.100+1.pem
# Convert to PKCS#12 keystore for Java:
openssl pkcs12 -export \
  -inkey 192.168.1.100+1-key.pem \
  -in 192.168.1.100+1.pem \
  -out server.p12 \
  -name moin \
  -passout pass:changeit



Option B: Plain self-signed (you’ll get a warning unless you import it)


keytool -genkeypair -alias moin -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 3650 \
  -storetype PKCS12 -keystore server.p12 -storepass changeit \
  -dname "CN=192.168.1.100" \
  -ext "SAN=IP:192.168.1.100"





Put 
server.p12
 into your Android project (e.g., 
app/src/main/res/raw/server.p12
).




2) Load the keystore and create an 
SSLServerSocket


Here’s a minimal HTTPS listener thread that mirrors your structure. It uses TLS 1.2+/1.3 and spawns your same 
ClientThread
 (which can remain unchanged; 
SSLSocket
 still provides 
getInputStream()
 / 
getOutputStream()
).


public class HttpsListenerThread extends Thread {
    private final SSLServerSocket serverSocket;

    public HttpsListenerThread(Context ctx) {
        try {
            char[] pass = "changeit".toCharArray(); // use your real password

            KeyStore ks = KeyStore.getInstance("PKCS12");
            try (InputStream is = ctx.getResources().openRawResource(R.raw.server)) {
                ks.load(is, pass);
            }

            KeyManagerFactory kmf = KeyManagerFactory.getInstance(
                    KeyManagerFactory.getDefaultAlgorithm());
            kmf.init(ks, pass);

            SSLContext sc = SSLContext.getInstance("TLS");
            sc.init(kmf.getKeyManagers(), null, null);

            SSLServerSocketFactory sf = sc.getServerSocketFactory();
            serverSocket = (SSLServerSocket) sf.createServerSocket(8443);

            // lock to modern protocols
            serverSocket.setEnabledProtocols(new String[] { "TLSv1.3", "TLSv1.2" });

            // (optional) narrow ciphers, require client auth, etc.

        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to init HTTPS server", e);
        }
    }

    @Override public void run() {
        try {
            for (;;) {
                SSLSocket s = (SSLSocket) serverSocket.accept();
                s.setUseClientMode(false);
                // Optional: force handshake now so errors show early
                // s.startHandshake();
                new ClientThread(s).start();
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            // log it
        }
    }
}



Start it like your HTTP version (prefer a different port, e.g., 8443):


listener = new HttpsListenerThread(getApplicationContext());
listener.start();



Your existing 
ClientThread
 can stay the same. It will read/write HTTP/1.1 bytes over the TLS stream.


3) Connect from the browser




Use 
https://192.168.1.100:8443/test




If you used 
mkcert
, install its local CA on the 
desktop
 you’re browsing from (mkcert’s 
-install
 already did this). No warning.




If you used a 
self-signed
 cert, the browser will warn. You can add a security exception (or import the cert into the OS/browser trust store).








Public CAs (e.g., Let’s Encrypt) generally 
won’t
 issue certs for raw private IPs. If you ever expose this externally with a DNS name you control, use a real CA via ACME and you’ll be warning-free for everyone.






Common gotchas




SAN missing
 → browsers reject with “not secure” even if CN matches.




Wrong host
 → the name (or IP) you type must be in SAN.




Android packaging
 → ensure 
server.p12
 ends up in 
res/raw
 and you load it with the correct resource id and password.




Permissions
 → your app needs 
android.permission.INTERNET
. (Binding to low ports like 443 may be restricted; stick to 8443.)




Keep-Alive
 → if you announce 
Connection: keep-alive
, either properly support multiple requests on one connection or switch to 
Connection: close
.




HTTP parsing
 → your demo is fine for a toy, but a real server should parse request lines/headers robustly.








Alternatives (if you don’t want to manage TLS yourself)




Terminate TLS on a reverse proxy
 (Caddy/Nginx) running on a PC/RPi on your LAN and proxy to your Android app’s HTTP on 
8082
. The proxy handles certs; your app stays simple.




Use a tiny embedded server that already supports TLS (e.g., 
NanoHTTPD
 has HTTPS support via keystore).

