from typing import Annotated
from fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, Header
from pydantic import BaseModel

app = FastAPI()

class CommonHeaders(BaseModel):
    simpleheader: str
    a_kebab_header: str | None

def get_common_headers(
    simpleheader: Annotated[str, Header()],
    a_kebab_header: str | None = Header(
        default=None,
        title="a-kebab-header",
        alias="a-kebab-header",
        description="This is a header that should be specified as `a-kebab-header`",
    ),
):
    return CommonHeaders(simpleheader=simpleheader, a_kebab_header=a_kebab_header)


@app.get("/")
def root_endpoint(
    headers: Annotated[CommonHeaders, Depends(get_common_headers)],
):
    result = {"headers received": headers}
    return result


@app.get("/another")
def another_endpoint(
    headers: Annotated[CommonHeaders, Depends(get_common_headers)],
):
    result = {"headers received": headers}
    return result

The docs
 only guarantee the following regarding memory consistency,




Memory consistency effects: Actions in a thread prior to the submission of a 
Runnable
 or 
Callable
 task to an 
ExecutorService
 happen-before any actions taken by that task, which in turn happen-before the result is retrieved via 
Future.get()
.




The assignment to 
scheduledFuture
 is not an action that is "prior to the submission of a 
Runnable
 or 
Callable
 task to an 
ExecutorService
". The assignment happens after the method has returned, by which time the task would have already been submitted.


Therefore, there is no happens-before relationship between the write to 
scheduledFuture
 and the "actions taken by that task", so it is possible for the code in 
doWork
 to not see the write.


This can be verified by running 
start
 in an infinite loop,


public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
    while (true) {
        new RaceCondition().start();
    }
}



with a slightly modified 
doWork
 to print something if a NPE is caught.


private void doWork()
{
    try {
        scheduledFuture.cancel(false);
    } catch (NullPointerException npe) {
        System.out.println("NULL!");
    }
    stop(); // shutdown the executor so that we don't run out of threads
}



