        // hq[2] -> id = 2
        // hq[3] -> null
        // hq[4] -> null

        hq[3] = hq[1]; // hq[3] now point to the same object as hq[1] (id = 1)
        hq[4] = hq[1]; // hq[4] also point to the same object as hq[1] (id = 1)

        hq[3] = null;  // Now hq[3] doesn’t point to anything anymore

        hq[4] = hq[0]; // hq[4] now point to the same object as hq[0] (id = 0)
        hq[0] = hq[3]; // hq[0] = null because hq[3] is null

        hq[3] = hq[2]; // hq[3] now point to the same object as hq[2] (id = 2)
        hq[2] = hq[0]; // hq[2] = null because hq[0] is null
    }

        // So at this point:
        // hq[0] -> null
        // hq[1] -> id = 1
        // hq[2] -> null
        // hq[3] -> id = 2
        // hq[4] -> id = 0
}

If I understand correctly, you're trying to test 
the extension itself
 to ensure it works correctly when used in your "real" tests. The JUnit Platform provides a 
test kit
 for this.




Example


For example, let's say we want to test the 
@SwingSetup
 annotation and 
EdtExtension
 class from 
my answer to your previous question
. You'd first write a test class that uses the extension. Then you'd write another test class that 
executes
 the previous test class using the 
JUnit Platform Test Kit
. And you'd set up your build (e.g., Maven, Gradle, etc.) so that only the latter test class is executed "for real".


